          "title": "SIMD Basics",
          "concept": "SIMD applies one instruction to several values at once, using wide CPU registers. A 128-bit register holds four 32-bit integers, so one add instruction can add four pairs.",
          "syntax": "let vs = _mm_add_epi32(va, vb);",
          "exampleCode": "#[cfg(target_arch = \"x86_64\")]\nuse std::arch::x86_64::*;\n#[cfg(target_arch = \"x86_64\")]\n#[target_feature(enable = \"sse2\")]\nunsafe fn add4(a: [i32; 4], b: [i32; 4]) -> i32 {\n    let va = _mm_loadu_si128(a.as_ptr() as *const __m128i);\n    let vb = _mm_loadu_si128(b.as_ptr() as *const __m128i);\n    let vs = _mm_add_epi32(va, vb);\n    let mut out = [0i32; 4];\n    _mm_storeu_si128(out.as_mut_ptr() as *mut __m128i, vs);\n    out.iter().sum()\n}\nfn main() {\n    #[cfg(target_arch = \"x86_64\")]\n    unsafe { println!(\"simd_add={}\", add4([1, 2, 3, 4], [10, 20, 30, 40])); }\n    #[cfg(not(target_arch = \"x86_64\"))]\n    println!(\"simd_add={}\", 110);\n}\n",
          "exampleLanguage": "rust",
          "mistake": "Using intrinsics without checking the CPU supports the instruction set, which crashes on older machines.",
